<DocGuide chapter="review" title="Architecture review" heading="Good boundaries." accent="Explicit remaining work." description="A review of the implemented dependency graph, ports, adapters, entities, value types and runtime responsibilities, with targeted fixes and a practical refactoring order." sections={sections}>
  <section id="verdict"><span class="eyebrow">01 / REVIEW CONCLUSION</span><h2>The separation is useful.<br />It is not finished everywhere.</h2>
    <p>The framework has real independent clients and avoids desktop/OS-service dependencies. The desktop receives adapters through port contracts, while production wiring lives in composition roots. Use cases depend only on the domain. These are meaningful boundaries, not just directory names.</p>
    <p>The domain is mostly typed data and small behavior models. Some types enforce invariants, while others are public records whose callers can construct invalid values. Launcher and widget interaction now belong to child controllers with explicit borrowed inputs. The root still coordinates shared snapshots, settings and bar/dock presentation. Message payload sizes remain application-owned, and service error handling is uneven. Those limits should guide the next changes rather than adding more empty crates or abstractions.</p>
    <p>The ongoing audit fixes concrete ownership and lifecycle problems, with separate evidence for unit tests, Vulkan pixels and native VM behavior. <a href={sourceUrl('docs/implementation-status.json')}>The implementation ledger</a> tracks the larger backlog and unverified paths. More crate names alone would not resolve those gaps.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="concepts"><span class="eyebrow">02 / NAME THE CONCEPT</span><h2>Entities, value types,<br />snapshots and policies.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Current examples</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Entity identity</td><td>Application + AppId, Window address, Workspace ID, Notification ID</td><td>AppId distinguishes desktop-entry identity from arbitrary strings. Other IDs remain strings/integers; stronger wrappers would prevent mixing them if used across more APIs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Validated value types</td><td>AppId, Placement, Date</td><td>Private storage and constructors enforce identity, finite coordinates and real calendar dates. Placement deserialization applies the same rule; Date derives its weekday instead of accepting a potentially inconsistent field.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Value records</td><td>LaunchCommand, LauncherSize</td><td>These describe values rather than independent identities. They retain public fields; launcher dimension checks occur at the settings boundary. Do not infer universal validation from their names.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Invariant-bearing value</td><td>Secret</td><td>Encapsulated storage, zeroization and no Debug/Clone reduce accidental copying or logging. This is a stronger boundary than a public String field.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stateful domain behavior</td><td>FocusTimer</td><td>Transitions and remaining time are explicit. Time is supplied as input, making behavior deterministic without system calls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service snapshot</td><td>CompositorSnapshot, SystemSnapshot, MediaSnapshot, WeatherSnapshot</td><td>Data returned by adapters. Transport and rendering details stay outside; error/unavailable states still need more consistent client handling.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Preference aggregate</td><td>DesktopSettings</td><td>Persisted preferences with backward-compatible defaults. Geometry resolution and design tokens stay in the client. Serde currently couples the domain record to the stored schema.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Use cases</td><td>Search ranking, activate application, move/snap widget, menu selection, notification inbox, validate settings</td><td>Pure policy or port calls. They do not launch processes, access files or render. Notification transport invokes the same policy as tests.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The new <code>LauncherSize</code> stores optional preferred dimensions. The settings validation use case rejects zero dimensions. The client enforces useful control sizes and screen/grid limits. Keeping screen pixels, theme tokens and compositor calls out of domain policy preserves the boundary.</p>
    <p><code>AppId::new</code> now enforces a non-empty desktop-file identity ending in <code>.desktop</code>, without path separators or control characters. Its storage is private and deserialization uses the same check; valid saved IDs keep their existing string representation. An invalid saved ID produces a settings error and preserves the original file. <code>Date::new</code> rejects impossible dates, derives weekdays and performs checked relative-month arithmetic. <code>Placement::new</code> and deserialization reject non-finite coordinates, while allowing negative positions. The client supplies grid spacing and screen bounds to use cases; those presentation rules are separate from finite-value validity.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="ports"><span class="eyebrow">03 / CONTRACTS AND IMPLEMENTATIONS</span><h2>Ports describe needs.<br />Adapters supply behavior.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Contract</th><th>Adapter</th><th>Implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ports.map(([port, contract, adapter, implementation]) => <tr><td>{port}</td><td>{contract}</td><td>{adapter}</td><td>{implementation}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p><code>DesktopAdapters</code> holds injected implementations through <code>Arc&lt;dyn …Port&gt;</code> handles. <code>desktop_adapters()</code> chooses concrete adapters. A trait-object call is an ordinary synchronous Rust call; the effects/subscription machinery decides which thread invokes it.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/platform.rs')}>desktop composition root</a> is the only desktop module selecting production service implementations. <a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/test_adapters.rs')}>In-memory adapters</a> let client tests exercise behavior without executing host commands. Lock and login have their own composition roots.</p>
    <p>No dedicated NetworkPort, BluetoothPort, BatteryPort, BrightnessPort or tray port exists yet. A network label in SystemSnapshot is not an implementation of network management. See <a href={withBase('/docs/system/')}>the system map</a> for retained tools and services.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="findings"><span class="eyebrow">04 / CONCRETE REVIEW RESULTS</span><h2>Fix the boundary.<br />Check the behavior.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Action / current status</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>CompositorPort contained a default two-second polling loop.</td><td>Removed the implementation from the domain contract. Hyprland owns watching/reconnects; the test adapter supplies its own behavior.</td><td>Domain desktop.rs; services compositor.rs; use-case fake.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The existing settings validation use case was not applied by JsonSettings.</td><td>Load and save now call it. Invalid saves fail before replacing the original file; optional launcher values preserve older settings.</td><td>Real file-adapter round-trip and rejection test.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Boundary tests missed nested modules and some Cargo declarations.</td><td>Source checks recurse through client/domain modules. The local dependency graph resolves package aliases and workspace inheritance across normal, build, development and target-specific declarations.</td><td>tests/test_architecture.py, including fixtures for bypasses the old checks missed. These checks do not analyze Rust semantics or registry-package internals.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The compositor adapter invented six workspaces for the bar.</td><td>Hyprland now reports actual workspaces, including special ones. The client builds six default shortcut buttons and adds other positive workspace IDs; narrow-layout rules keep the active destination visible.</td><td>Adapter snapshot fixture, client workspace policy and bar geometry tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Date allowed day zero and conflicting weekdays; Placement could carry NaN.</td><td>Constructors now enforce both invariants. Calendar month arithmetic is constant-time domain behavior. Invalid drag dimensions are rejected before mutation; release clears transient drag state even on failure. Persisted-settings failures retain their own error state through unrelated successful effects.</td><td>Calendar checks against chrono across two 400-year cycles, invalid deserialization, persistence compatibility, extreme snapping and drag tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shadow dimensions were fixed in the GPU shader.</td><td>The public Shadow value carries color, offset and blur. The design system supplies elevation tokens; layout emits a distinct shadow primitive and the shader derives falloff and extent from its parameters.</td><td>1×/2× Vulkan tests vary blur independently of the renderer. Desktop visual regression suite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Primitive corners differed between software and hardware Vulkan.</td><td>The renderer now selects fine derivatives explicitly for primitive and rounded-clip antialiasing. This fixes driver-dependent coverage without teaching the renderer client styling.</td><td>All 148 existing visual comparisons pass on both drivers with unchanged baselines and tolerances; focused mirror-symmetry checks cover 1×/2× and both pixel-quad phases.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Launcher dimensions were fixed presentation constants.</td><td>Added persisted preferences and token defaults. Client geometry resolves them; neither domain nor renderer learns about launcher placement.</td><td>Resizing, keyboard, migration, clipping and native visual tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wallpaper selection committed paths before decoding, and local/online intent could race.</td><td>A child coordinator prepares immutable snapshots, rejects stale preparation and finishes accepted commits in order. The visible background uses the prepared image. Explicit palette choices supersede older extraction.</td><td>Storage, cancellation, coalescing and desktop integration tests; native evidence tracked in the implementation ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Desktop owns launcher, bar and widget state in one struct.</td><td>Launcher query, mode, selection, scrolling, event routing, selector rendering and panel motion are now owned by a child with private state. Borrowed inputs and typed intents connect it to the root. Widget drag/calendar/timer state and views now have the same boundary, with one catalog supplying selector metadata, geometry and rendering. Bar/dock coordination remains open.</td><td>launcher/ and widgets/; child behavior tests, boundary guards and the production visual suite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One effects worker delayed unrelated actions.</td><td>Named bounded queues now separate persistence, assets, exports and interactive actions. Tests exercise ordering, independence, cancellation, saturation and worker panic. Runtime delivery now bounds waiting messages at 64, applies producer backpressure and rejects queued events from cancelled subscription lifetimes. Live subscription bodies are capped at 64, including streams still stopping.</td><td>lucent-wayland run_inner and lucent-api Effects.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>IPC cleanup was only on the normal run_inner exit path.</td><td>An owned command server now cancels and joins its worker on scope exit, including early errors. Its poller wake-up is independent of the socket path, and cleanup checks the recorded file identity. Connected I/O has absolute deadlines; blocked delivery and pending replies observe cancellation.</td><td>Isolated socket tests cover error returns/retry, missing/replaced paths, saturated delivery, pending replies, slow peers and quit acknowledgement. Native evidence is tracked separately in the implementation ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Every notes edit queues a full settings snapshot; completion shares the generic action-result message.</td><td>A client persistence coordinator now tracks revisions, allows one outstanding settings task and keeps only the latest pending snapshot. It retains failures across unrelated results and supports retry through Notes or IPC. Notes expose unavailable, saving, retrying, saved and failed states.</td><td>persistence.rs: burst coalescing, delayed completion, failure/retry, rejected work and stale results; root/widget status and retry tests. Native evidence is tracked in the implementation ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Widget dragging changes the shared preference aggregate before release.</td><td>The host now keeps a separate bounded preview and commits through the move use case only on release. Reset, hide and invalid input cancel the rest of a gesture. A drag begins at the currently visible position even if a saved position falls outside a resized output.</td><td>Injected-adapter regression verifies an unrelated save excludes the preview and the release persists the final snap. Cancellation and clamped-origin behavior have child tests; native evidence is tracked separately in the ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wallpaper integration tests relied on a rollback snapshot still being in the cache.</td><td>A test checkpoint now preserves the selected owned image and metadata outside the cache. The helper restores it before IPC rollback, refuses to overwrite changed content, and removes private test storage after restoration is verified. Production cache policy is unchanged.</td><td>Pruned/present/changed/corrupt/symlink and legacy-file tests; native wallpaper transaction and both provider workflows with original-desktop restoration.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Offscreen snapshots multiplied dimensions without overflow checks; output paths did not check device texture limits.</td><td>A renderer-owned extent validator checks logical-to-physical multiplication and the requested device's texture limit. Snapshots also validate padded readback size against buffer and host limits before GPU work. Zero-sized native surfaces retain retry behavior.</td><td>Boundary arithmetic tests and a Vulkan rejection-then-valid-render regression. This protects output allocation inputs, not all scene resources or device-memory availability.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An opaque 65×3 image lost alpha at its final pixel on software Vulkan; exact fractional clip edges disagreed with hit testing.</td><td>Clipping now happens after derivative evaluation and implicit texture sampling. Clip coordinates come from the physical sample center, and right/bottom edges use the same exclusion rule as Rect::contains.</td><td>The original opaque pixel returned alpha 215 instead of 255 on llvmpipe; RADV passed. Focused regressions now cover odd output sizes, fractional clip boundaries, transparent exterior pixels and 1×/2× density. Existing visual tolerances remain unchanged.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Output validation did not cover scene content.</td><td>The renderer now checks finite geometry, image dimensions, text requests and geometry-buffer capacity against its numeric envelope and actual device limits. Text preparation checks glyph bitmap dimensions before rasterization; packed scene buffers use fallible reservation. Canvas::paint returns an error before geometry writes or frame submission if preparation fails.</td><td>Pure boundary checks and an offscreen GPU regression cover early rejection, a later glyph failure after an image upload, retained previous pixels/resources, and subsequent valid rendering. Upstream layout, font parsing, available memory and device-fault recovery remain separate boundaries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notification delivery had an unbounded signal queue and no idle disconnect observation.</td><td>The adapter now admits up to 256 pending signals before changing state. Two-signal actions and timeout batches honor that capacity. A closed connection reports unavailability and triggers reconnection; pending signals from the ended connection are discarded. Exclusive name acquisition refuses to displace another server. Inbox/history remain in memory.</td><td>Private-daemon tests reproduced both failures and now pass stalled-subscriber overload/recovery, normal protocol/actions, idle bus restart and name contention. Adapter tests cover atomic rejection, expiration capacity and connection cleanup. One drained batch can coexist with the next queue; this is not durable delivery or a total memory bound.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Some discovery/provider failures become empty/default values.</td><td>Still open. Model loading, empty, unavailable and failed separately where the UI needs to explain them.</td><td>platform.rs and desktop initialization/subscriptions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One shared viewport and generic surface IDs limit per-output behavior.</td><td>Still open. Introduce an output-aware desktop model before claiming robust independent multi-monitor layouts.</td><td>Desktop.viewport and runtime surface reconciliation.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Tests verify boundaries and selected behaviors; they do not prove every operating-system feature works. Native visual tests use the real renderer, while hardware, login and secure-lock testing require separate environments.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-render-extents.json')}>renderer validation report</a> records eight focused GPU tests and 172 unchanged visual comparisons on each of the software and hardware drivers, plus native desktop checks. Derivatives and implicit texture sampling depend on neighboring fragment invocations, as described by the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/#derivatives">WGSL execution model</a>. Moving discard fixed the reproduced driver difference; the test does not identify a specific compiler instruction defect or establish correctness on every driver. Late discard can execute shader work for clipped fragments, so this change makes no performance-improvement claim.</p>
    <h3>Where failures cross the boundaries</h3>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-notification-delivery.json')}>notification delivery report</a> records three reproduced protocol failures, four passing private-daemon regressions and native replacement/action/dismiss/expiration checks. Name contention and bus restart are confined to test-owned daemons. The VM check uses transient fixtures and real pointer clicks; it does not restart the desktop bus. Persistent history, rich content and consistent service-status presentation remain separate work.</p>
    <p>The subsequent <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-render-scenes.json')}>scene-validation report</a> records nine focused renderer checks and all 172 unchanged visual comparisons on both drivers. Its rejection test retains previous pixels and pinned resources after both early validation and later glyph-preparation errors, then renders a valid next frame. Native launcher, restart and existing secure lock/unlock checks pass. The runtime still treats a render error as fatal; offscreen recovery does not establish native device-loss recovery or a process-memory bound.</p>
    <p><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/desktop.rs')}>Desktop's message handling</a> currently ignores a failed system sample, replaces failed media reads with a default snapshot and turns failed weather reads into an absent value. This loses the distinction between an empty result and an unavailable service. The adapters also need scrutiny: a missing volume read already becomes a default inside <a href={sourceUrl('lucent/crates/lucent-services/src/lib.rs')}>SystemProbe</a>, before the client sees it. Adding a generic error banner only at the root would leave that ambiguity intact.</p>
    <p>The next service slice should preserve a typed outcome from the adapter, retain the last successful sample when useful, expose freshness/failure in client state and verify recovery after disconnect. Domain contracts describe service meaning; adapters own protocol/reconnect details; components decide how to present that state. A port-shaped trait by itself does not establish those semantics.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="performance"><span class="eyebrow">05 / MEASURED COST AND OPEN QUESTIONS</span><h2>Low idle CPU.<br />Memory still needs work.</h2>
    <p>The latest two-minute runs measured <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache-before-120.json')}>588.26 MiB mean RSS before lazy previews</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache-after-120.json')}>389.93 MiB afterward</a>: an observed reduction of 198.33 MiB. The initial 200 MiB target remains unmet. Detected display, workload, adapter, vCPU, memory and kernel metadata match; these are sequential runs on a shared host, not an isolated allocation-attribution experiment.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>120-second sample</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Mean process RSS</td><td>588.26 MiB</td><td>389.93 MiB</td><td>Lower measured residency; source and executable hashes are in each report.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Main-process CPU, one core</td><td>0.700%</td><td>0.825%</td><td>No CPU improvement claimed. Including service helpers: 1.460% and 1.733%.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Idle background / closed-dock frames</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>Both remain event-driven; visible widgets still update their data.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Client prepared pixels after idle</td><td>Not instrumented</td><td>59.8 MiB</td><td>48.0 MiB background, 11.8 MiB icons, no local/online previews. Other owners and driver allocations are excluded.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-preview-cache.json')}>native cache test</a> browsed a generated 73-image library, reached beyond the old preload limit, recovered a corrupt preview through retry, and verified the smaller closed-browser budget. Both live providers supplied thumbnails without changing the wallpaper or palette. This establishes the tested ownership/lifecycle behavior; it does not establish hardware frame pacing or total GPU memory.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Implication</th><th>Next test / owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Prepared images, active GPU textures, caches and surface buffers have different lifetimes.</td><td>The unused texture-cache budget does not cap process RSS. Active scene resources remain pinned, and driver allocations are outside the reported payload counters.</td><td>Measure app asset ownership alongside renderer payloads and Linux mappings. Asset policy belongs in presentation adapters; GPU retention belongs in the renderer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NativeAssets previously prepared up to 64 local previews at startup; backgrounds still prepare up to 4096 pixels.</td><td>Local previews now load on demand. Background source sizing remains a candidate for reduction; current changes keep the existing preview density.</td><td>Verify native browsing, repeated cache turnover and before/after resource measurements. Keep output-aware preparation separate from decoding in view or paint.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Selected wallpaper previews previously accumulated in Desktop.images without eviction.</td><td>A presentation coordinator now bounds local preview demand/entries, evicts unwanted images and reduces reuse after closing. Application-owned prepared storage has deduplicated byte accounting; in-flight work and other owners are excluded.</td><td>Rapid-navigation, retry, stale-result/transaction precedence, bounded retention and accounting tests passed, along with native browsing/retry checks. The paired RSS reports show the observed process change; disk snapshot retention and GPU cache lifetimes remain separate.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A profiled cold launcher opening had a 101.35 ms submission gap; its largest recorded dock frame cost was 10.63 ms.</td><td>Successful dock-frame work alone does not explain the gap. A later same-binary normal run had a 24.25 ms cold maximum; that does not prove the earlier hitch fixed.</td><td>Correlate input, update, reconciliation, other surfaces and submission timestamps. Keep profiling in runtime/renderer, with bounded storage and no idle redraws.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text rasterization still runs synchronously on a cache miss.</td><td>Warm text can be cheap while a new query, font or scale incurs preparation work. Cache-hit behavior cannot establish first-use responsiveness.</td><td>Measure realistic large application lists and font/scale transitions before choosing asynchronous text preparation or prewarming.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hardware and software Vulkan pass the existing visual suite after explicit fine-derivative coverage.</td><td>This establishes selected pixel correctness within tolerance, not native hardware frame pacing, power use or GPU execution time.</td><td>Run the actual desktop on hardware and test fractional scaling, hotplug and sleep/resume separately.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-frame-profile-120.json')}>profiled report</a> stores bounded CPU wall times. Text rasterization and texture upload are nested inside preparation; adding those numbers would double-count work. Submission/presentation may include driver waits. They are not Vulkan GPU timestamps or monitor scanout measurements.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="gaps"><span class="eyebrow">06 / IMPLEMENTATION COVERAGE</span><h2>A working shell.<br />An incomplete desktop platform.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Current limit</th><th>Required boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Control center and tray</td><td>Native network setup, Bluetooth pairing, device routing and StatusNotifier item/menu lifecycles remain absent. Summary labels and volume actions do not cover these use cases.</td><td>Typed domain snapshots and commands; explicit ports; platform adapters handling service lifecycle; usable client controls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text and accessibility</td><td>General caret/selection editing, grapheme behavior, complex-script shaping, font fallback, IME and accessibility integration remain incomplete.</td><td>Reusable input/layout/rendering contracts and platform protocol integration, exercised by multiple clients.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Displays and widgets</td><td>One shared viewport limits independent per-monitor layouts. Resizing, fractional scale and hotplug need a coherent output model and native validation.</td><td>Output identity in framework surface contracts; persisted per-output placement and grid policy in the desktop.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Launcher and dock</td><td>Child ownership is improved, but app-install discovery updates, interactive pin/reorder and autohide remain separate work.</td><td>Application discovery/watch behind its port; launcher/dock behavior in the client; persistence through settings use cases.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notifications and service health</td><td>History is in memory; rich content/grouping and consistent loading/error/reconnect presentation remain incomplete. Notes now show acknowledged save state; service loading and reconnect presentation remains uneven.</td><td>Pure lifecycle/storage policy, protocol adapters and typed client states. Keep transport details out of views.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>These are tracked requirements, not features implied by the screenshots. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/implementation-status.json')}>implementation ledger</a> records acceptance criteria and evidence. Root-greeter interactive login and driver-fault injection also remain explicitly unverified.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="next"><span class="eyebrow">07 / REFACTORING ORDER</span><h2>Use implemented use cases<br />to justify each abstraction.</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps"><li>Extend the tested notification delivery boundary into cohesive service status and controls. Preserve unavailable/error outcomes through ports and adapters instead of converting them to empty data.</li><li>Apply the measured preview lessons to remaining ownership: online pages retain a full page, and the background still prepares a 4096-pixel source. Output-aware preparation needs a real output/density contract first.</li><li>Keep renderer admission limits separate from memory budgets and native recovery policy. Extend upstream layout and text contracts through concrete input use cases, without treating offscreen rejection tests as device-loss evidence.</li><li>Add output identity and per-output geometry, then verify scaling and hotplug in a real compositor session.</li><li>Continue applying explicit inputs and typed intents where bar/dock state warrants a separate owner. Strengthen domain values where invalid inputs reach persistence or external commands, preserving migration behavior.</li><li>Replace retained stock capabilities one at a time through a port, adapter, usable client and rollback test. Keep existing native shell ownership and secure rollback intact.</li></ol>
    <p>For changes, use the repository's <a href={sourceUrl('.agents/skills/lucent-framework/SKILL.md')}>framework skill</a> or <a href={sourceUrl('.agents/skills/lucent-desktop/SKILL.md')}>desktop/integration skill</a>. The <a href={withBase('/docs/library/')}>library guide</a> and <a href={withBase('/docs/desktop/')}>user configuration guide</a> keep implementation concepts separate from everyday settings.</p>
  </section>
</DocGuide>
